  "decisions": [
    [
      "ANSWERED - the two stores are dropped",
      "Sean, 2026-09-09. found-colony delivers a garrison and two extractors.",
      "3 metal-equivalent, which is exactly an ark's Binding and a pioneer's, so deploy ark and found by land both conserve exactly."
    ],
    [
      "ANSWERED - how to catch an infinite-resource glitch",
      "Sean, 2026-09-08, asking whether unbounded capacity means specification errors cannot be detected.",
      "It does not. Structural boundedness is polynomial by linear programming, at edit time, with a witness - see Detecting a glitch. Boundedness for one starting state is EXPSPACE-complete; boundedness for every starting state is polynomial, and the second is the one an editor needs."
    ],
    [
      "OPEN - the six lines beside it",
      "Soft on the garrison alone still creates 2 citizens, 2 extractors and 2 stores on a territory that already has them, because their capacities are not zero.",
      "Marked ? in found-colony. Each needs hard, soft, or removing - and they are not the same answer."
    ],
    [
      "OPEN - or make the call soft instead, and answer all seven at once",
      "call found-colony(t) [soft] means deploying onto a founded territory destroys the ark and does nothing else. Soft on each create means deploy tops up a damaged colony.",
      "Those are different games. The model expresses both; attachment is on the call as readily as on a create."
    ],
    [
      "OPEN - if a call can fail as a unit, does it roll back?",
      "found-colony can now half-succeed. If the ark is destroyed before the call, a soft call that skips leaves the ark spent for nothing.",
      "This is the same question as whether composition sees the starting state or the running one."
    ],
    [
      "OPEN - do the sketch's missing stores come back?",
      "The sketch creates a garrison, 2 citizens and 2 extractors. The specification also creates a food store and a metal store.",
      "Dropping them changes the game."
    ],
    [
      "OPEN - does a thing have identity, or is it a count?",
      "set requires an identified thing. destroy ark means this ark. create citizen 2 asks for no identity.",
      "The dump already answers this: nine of its ten tables are the containment tree of identified things."
    ]
  ],
