  "invariants": [
    [
      "metal",
      "conserved",
      "Kinds: \"what things are built from; conserved\"",
      "P-invariant: every formula's net effect on metal is zero. EXACT - no false alarms.",
      "Recommended first. The declaration already exists as prose and nothing checks it."
    ],
    [
      "energy",
      "neither conserved nor expiring",
      "Kinds: stated in those words",
      "Declared free to grow, so excluded from the conservation check by the author's own words.",
      "Nothing to check, and saying so is the point - the check knows what it is not looking at."
    ],
    [
      "food",
      "expires",
      "Kinds: \"eaten by citizens; expires\"",
      "keeps 1, so it cannot accumulate across turns. A separate property from conservation.",
      "The growth loop lives here and is intended."
    ],
    [
      "citizen",
      "grows on surplus",
      "the automatic food recipe holds it in check",
      "Deliberately unbounded. A structural check WILL flag this loop, correctly.",
      "Which is why the broad check has to be a diff against declared intent, not an absolute."
    ]
  ],
  "detection": [
    [
      "1. Declared conservation",
      "P-invariant: is there a weighting under which this resource's net change is zero across every formula?",
      "Polynomial. EXACT - it cannot cry wolf.",
      "Names the formula that breaks it.",
      "Targets exactly \"I accidentally made metal from nothing\". The spec already declares which resources are conserved; nothing checks it."
    ],
    [
      "2. Structural unboundedness",
      "T-increasing: is there a non-negative firing vector x, not all zero, with C.x >= 0?",
      "Polynomial by linear programming. CONSERVATIVE - it ignores guards, so it can flag a loop the guards prevent, and cannot miss one.",
      "The firing vector IS the witness: which formulas, in what ratio.",
      "Catches loops nobody declared. Must be reported as a diff against intended loops, because the citizen economy is genuinely a T-increasing."
    ],
    [
      "3. A large generic cap at runtime",
      "Stop and report if any count passes a big number.",
      "Cheap. Detects after the fact, in one playthrough, at whatever numbers that run happened to hit.",
      "Says a number got large. Says nothing about which formulas did it.",
      "Keep as a backstop against bugs in checks 1 and 2. Never as the mechanism - a cap that fires tells you nothing about why."
    ]
  ],
